Red at 917c70e (a-verb-phrase-read-as-a-column):
  "NEGATIVE — TWO columns still outrank the noun, exactly as before"
  "NEGATIVE — and the archetype that a column list SHOULD override still is"
  detectAppKind('عندي عيادة أسنان، أريد جدولاً فيه اسم المريض ورقم تلفونه ووقت الموعد')
    Expected: "generic"  Received: "booking"

Culprit (bisected): 81e8913c. detectAppKind's "a list he wrote outranks a
noun he happened to use" rule now goes through hasExplicitRecordSchema,
which also requires a build clause. 76f66a5d then made that gate
deliberate - it reads a build verb at a clause start ("I run a camel farm.
I want a register…") and pins detectAppKind(<camel description with no
build verb>) === null. So the gate stays.
Root cause: the clause boundary was read only at [.؟!\n]. The owner closes
the context clause with «،» just as often; his request «أريد جدولاً …»
stood after a comma, went unread, and the clinic noun won the classifier.
Decision: R, inside 76f66a5d's own design: «،» also starts a clause for the
Arabic build words (first-person wants / imperatives). English keeps its
sentence marks: there the comma separates list items and «make»/«design»
after it may be a column. 76f66a5d's camel test, the planner's "a noun
phrase is a specification, not a request" test and engineering-discovery
stay green. Added the negative: the same clinic sentence with the verb
removed («، جدولها فيه …») is not promoted.

Mutation evidence (mutate.py, sha256 identical after each):
  M1c «،» removed from the Arabic clause start:
    ✕ NEGATIVE — TWO columns still outrank the noun  Expected: "generic" Received: "booking"
    ✕ NEGATIVE — and the archetype that a column list SHOULD override still is
      Expected: "generic" Received: "booking"
  M2c any «،» counts as a request (verb no longer required):
    ✕ NEGATIVE — the «،» opens his request only through his verb
      Expected: not "generic"

